{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01G8EmcXfoRADzLTN3DEs4uY","type":"tool_result","content":"/workspace/paper/.claude/skills/validate-paper/templates/figure_manifest.schema.json\n34:\\begin{abstract}\n41:\\end{abstract}\n35:Delayed generalization (``grokking'') on modular arithmetic is explained through memorization capacity, weight norm, circuit efficiency, or a lazy-to-rich transition, but its dependence on model size has received little systematic measurement.\n40:The evaluation itself was valid, so the result is a calibrated non-answer rather than a failure; we state what it cannot say and which measurements would resolve it.\n45:In small algorithmic tasks such as modular addition, networks often fit the training split almost immediately and generalize to held-out pairs only after much longer training \\citep{Power2022GrokkingGB}. Several accounts of this delay have been proposed: a mechanistic account in which a generalizing circuit forms gradually under weight decay \\citep{Nanda2023ProgressMF}, a loss-landscape and weight-norm account \\citep{Liu2022OmnigrokGB}, a circuit-efficiency account \\citep{Varma2023ExplainingGT}, and a lazy-to-rich account of training dynamics \\citep{Kumar2023GrokkingAT}. How the delay depends on \\emph{model size} is a more practical question: it constrains these accounts and informs which model sizes are worth using in experiments on the phenomenon. A capacity-based reading predicts that wider models memorize sooner, and, if generalization time is less sensitive to width, that the delay changes systematically with width.\n229:For context, three configurations that differ cumulatively by the three modifications of Section~\\ref{sec:model} were evaluated on the separate development plane with two seeds each, with scores $0.798$, $0.828$, and $0.850$ and $4$, $6$, and $4$ of $6$ runs censored (Appendix~\\ref{sec:si-dev}). Differences between them are of the same size as the spread between the two seeds of any single configuration, the plane differs from the sealed one in modulus, training fraction, widths, step limit, and seeds, and no slope interval was computed; we therefore draw no inference from them.\n235:\\paragraph{Relation to the explanations of grokking.} Each account in Section~\\ref{sec:related} associates the delay with a quantity that can depend on width or parametrization: memorization capacity (how early the training split is fitted), initial weight norm and the loss landscape, the relative efficiency of memorizing and generalizing circuits, and the position on the lazy-to-rich axis. The evaluated model touches the last two knobs directly: its initial weights are shrunk by half, which the source comments describe as aimed at reducing lazy-regime memorization, and its multipliers change how large a step each layer takes at a given width. A clear width slope from this model could therefore reflect the multipliers rather than width alone, and a flat slope could reflect their compensating a stock-model dependence. This is why the isolating comparison with a matched stock model (Section~\\ref{sec:discussion}, item iv) matters more than a further estimate of the slope for the evaluated model alone.\n350:Figure~\\ref{fig:dev} shows the three development configurations: V1 (smaller initial weights and last-position-only computation), V2 (V1 plus width-aware output scaling), and V3 (V2 plus hidden-layer reparametrization; the evaluated model). Scores are means over two seeds on the development plane; per-seed scores are plotted. Table~\\ref{tab:si-dev} adds the mean final held-out accuracy and the censoring counts. The rank order by score is reversed relative to the end-of-training accuracy: the mean final held-out accuracy decreases from V1 to V3 ($0.981$, $0.944$, $0.921$) while the score increases ($0.798$, $0.828$, $0.850$), so the score and the end-of-training accuracy measure different things. All six V2 runs are censored although their mean final held-out accuracy is $0.944$; the retained records do not show whether the censoring came from a missed training threshold or a missed held-out threshold, or how close individual runs came to $0.99$. The per-seed scores of V3 ($0.801$, $0.899$) span the V2 mean ($0.828$) and lie just above the V1 mean ($0.798$). With two seeds per configuration, the seed-to-seed spread ($0.051$, $0.043$, $0.098$) is as large as or larger than the differences between configuration means ($0.030$, $0.022$), so no ordering of the configurations is supported.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cdd964e4-3ba4-4c45-969d-584fa83d6aeb","uuid":"68538e6d-e363-41b9-9b1a-c0599b3e4c91","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:49:19.441Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"/workspace/paper/.claude/skills/validate-paper/templates/figure_manifest.schema.json\n34:\\begin{abstract}\n41:\\end{abstract}\n35:Delayed generalization (``grokking'') on modular arithmetic is explained through memorization capacity, weight norm, circuit efficiency, or a lazy-to-rich transition, but its dependence on model size has received little systematic measurement.\n40:The evaluation itself was valid, so the result is a calibrated non-answer rather than a failure; we state what it cannot say and which measurements would resolve it.\n45:In small algorithmic tasks such as modular addition, networks often fit the training split almost immediately and generalize to held-out pairs only after much longer training \\citep{Power2022GrokkingGB}. Several accounts of this delay have been proposed: a mechanistic account in which a generalizing circuit forms gradually under weight decay \\citep{Nanda2023ProgressMF}, a loss-landscape and weight-norm account \\citep{Liu2022OmnigrokGB}, a circuit-efficiency account \\citep{Varma2023ExplainingGT}, and a lazy-to-rich account of training dynamics \\citep{Kumar2023GrokkingAT}. How the delay depends on \\emph{model size} is a more practical question: it constrains these accounts and informs which model sizes are worth using in experiments on the phenomenon. A capacity-based reading predicts that wider models memorize sooner, and, if generalization time is less sensitive to width, that the delay changes systematically with width.\n229:For context, three configurations that differ cumulatively by the three modifications of Section~\\ref{sec:model} were evaluated on the separate development plane with two seeds each, with scores $0.798$, $0.828$, and $0.850$ and $4$, $6$, and $4$ of $6$ runs censored (Appendix~\\ref{sec:si-dev}). Differences between them are of the same size as the spread between the two seeds of any single configuration, the plane differs from the sealed one in modulus, training fraction, widths, step limit, and seeds, and no slope interval was computed; we therefore draw no inference from them.\n235:\\paragraph{Relation to the explanations of grokking.} Each account in Section~\\ref{sec:related} associates the delay with a quantity that can depend on width or parametrization: memorization capacity (how early the training split is fitted), initial weight norm and the loss landscape, the relative efficiency of memorizing and generalizing circuits, and the position on the lazy-to-rich axis. The evaluated model touches the last two knobs directly: its initial weights are shrunk by half, which the source comments describe as aimed at reducing lazy-regime memorization, and its multipliers change how large a step each layer takes at a given width. A clear width slope from this model could therefore reflect the multipliers rather than width alone, and a flat slope could reflect their compensating a stock-model dependence. This is why the isolating comparison with a matched stock model (Section~\\ref{sec:discussion}, item iv) matters more than a further estimate of the slope for the evaluated model alone.\n350:Figure~\\ref{fig:dev} shows the three development configurations: V1 (smaller initial weights and last-position-only computation), V2 (V1 plus width-aware output scaling), and V3 (V2 plus hidden-layer reparametrization; the evaluated model). Scores are means over two seeds on the development plane; per-seed scores are plotted. Table~\\ref{tab:si-dev} adds the mean final held-out accuracy and the censoring counts. The rank order by score is reversed relative to the end-of-training accuracy: the mean final held-out accuracy decreases from V1 to V3 ($0.981$, $0.944$, $0.921$) while the score increases ($0.798$, $0.828$, $0.850$), so the score and the end-of-training accuracy measure different things. All six V2 runs are censored although their mean final held-out accuracy is $0.944$; the retained records do not show whether the censoring came from a missed training threshold or a missed held-out threshold, or how close individual runs came to $0.99$. The per-seed scores of V3 ($0.801$, $0.899$) span the V2 mean ($0.828$) and lie just above the V1 mean ($0.798$). With two seeds per configuration, the seed-to-seed spread ($0.051$, $0.043$, $0.098$) is as large as or larger than the differences between configuration means ($0.030$, $0.022$), so no ordering of the configurations is supported.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
