{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgwKs3cohMpRbJ7P9pFD2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QgPGC647yESV3cunCejxYE","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; sed -n 34,41p paper.tex; grep -n \"Grokking and its explanations\" paper.tex; grep -n \"none of them\\|measured delay against width\" paper.tex; grep -n \"sec:si-dev\" paper.tex | head -3; sed -n 338,349p paper.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1855,"cache_read_input_tokens":53134,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1855},"output_tokens":32,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cdd964e4-3ba4-4c45-969d-584fa83d6aeb","uuid":"a1fc4f91-05c7-4beb-aff8-882263fda050","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:49:21.349Z","request_id":"req_011CgwKs36JiLyUt1mzv3CYM"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QgPGC647yESV3cunCejxYE","type":"tool_result","content":"\\begin{abstract}\nDelayed generalization (``grokking'') on modular arithmetic is explained through memorization capacity, weight norm, circuit efficiency, or a lazy-to-rich transition, but its dependence on model size has received little systematic measurement.\nWe test one falsifiable prediction in a one-layer transformer trained with AdamW (weight decay $1.0$, full batch) on modular addition modulo $97$: that the delay between fitting the training pairs and generalizing to held-out pairs shrinks as width grows.\nDelay is right-censored at $10{,}000$ steps, and a pre-declared rule reads the 95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval of the slope of $\\ln(\\text{delay})$ against $\\log_2(\\text{width})$ over widths $16$--$256$ with four seeds.\nThe measured slope is $-0.020$ with interval $[-0.585,\\,0.354]$, so the verdict is \\emph{inconclusive}; $40\\%$ of the $20$ runs were censored, and the interval covers about two-fifths of the slopes that the delay floor and cap permit.\nA single-seed reference trainer gives $-0.368$, inside the interval.\nThe evaluation itself was valid, so the result is a calibrated non-answer rather than a failure; we state what it cannot say and which measurements would resolve it.\n\\end{abstract}\n62:\\paragraph{Grokking and its explanations.} \\citet{Power2022GrokkingGB} report that small transformers trained on algorithmically generated tables, including modular arithmetic, reach high held-out accuracy long after fitting the training split. \\citet{Nanda2023ProgressMF} analyze small transformers trained on modular addition mechanistically and define progress measures that track the formation of the generalizing circuit during training. \\citet{Liu2022OmnigrokGB} analyze loss landscapes and weight norm and extend the phenomenon beyond algorithmic data; \\citet{Varma2023ExplainingGT} explain grokking through circuit efficiency and test predictions of that account in training experiments; \\citet{Kumar2023GrokkingAT} relate it to a transition from lazy to rich training dynamics, analyzed in a two-layer regression setting; \\citet{Huang2024UnifiedVO} discuss grokking, double descent, and emergent abilities through the competition of circuits; and \\citet{Davies2023UnifyingGA} place grokking and double descent in one framework. These accounts differ in which quantity should control the delay, and several involve scale-related knobs. Empirical studies have varied some of those knobs on modular arithmetic or related algorithmic tasks: \\citet{Doshi2023ToGO} study modular arithmetic with corrupted labels and examine how generalization and memorization coexist under regularization; \\citet{Thilak2022TheSM} associate grokking under adaptive optimizers with cyclic behavior of the last-layer norm; and \\citet{Prieto2025GrokkingAT} attribute the absence of grokking without regularization to numerical instability in the softmax. We do not re-derive these findings, and none of them has, as far as their stated scope and our non-exhaustive reading indicate, measured delay against width under a fixed protocol, which is the quantity examined here; the present study does not test the accounts against each other.\n62:\\paragraph{Grokking and its explanations.} \\citet{Power2022GrokkingGB} report that small transformers trained on algorithmically generated tables, including modular arithmetic, reach high held-out accuracy long after fitting the training split. \\citet{Nanda2023ProgressMF} analyze small transformers trained on modular addition mechanistically and define progress measures that track the formation of the generalizing circuit during training. \\citet{Liu2022OmnigrokGB} analyze loss landscapes and weight norm and extend the phenomenon beyond algorithmic data; \\citet{Varma2023ExplainingGT} explain grokking through circuit efficiency and test predictions of that account in training experiments; \\citet{Kumar2023GrokkingAT} relate it to a transition from lazy to rich training dynamics, analyzed in a two-layer regression setting; \\citet{Huang2024UnifiedVO} discuss grokking, double descent, and emergent abilities through the competition of circuits; and \\citet{Davies2023UnifyingGA} place grokking and double descent in one framework. These accounts differ in which quantity should control the delay, and several involve scale-related knobs. Empirical studies have varied some of those knobs on modular arithmetic or related algorithmic tasks: \\citet{Doshi2023ToGO} study modular arithmetic with corrupted labels and examine how generalization and memorization coexist under regularization; \\citet{Thilak2022TheSM} associate grokking under adaptive optimizers with cyclic behavior of the last-layer norm; and \\citet{Prieto2025GrokkingAT} attribute the absence of grokking without regularization to numerical instability in the softmax. We do not re-derive these findings, and none of them has, as far as their stated scope and our non-exhaustive reading indicate, measured delay against width under a fixed protocol, which is the quantity examined here; the present study does not test the accounts against each other.\n115:The retained final artifacts consist of the aggregate metrics listed in Section~\\ref{sec:results} and one record per seed (mean validation-accuracy score and wall-clock time). \\emph{Per-width and per-seed delays, fitting and generalization steps, and per-width censoring flags are not retained}, so we cannot show a delay-versus-width plot, and we do not infer one. Separately, a development evaluation on a different data plane (modulus $53$, training fraction $0.5$, widths $32,64,128$, $6{,}000$ steps, two seeds) was used when configuring the evaluated model; its results are reported only as descriptive context in Appendix~\\ref{sec:si-dev} and never mixed with the sealed result.\n229:For context, three configurations that differ cumulatively by the three modifications of Section~\\ref{sec:model} were evaluated on the separate development plane with two seeds each, with scores $0.798$, $0.828$, and $0.850$ and $4$, $6$, and $4$ of $6$ runs censored (Appendix~\\ref{sec:si-dev}). Differences between them are of the same size as the spread between the two seeds of any single configuration, the plane differs from the sealed one in modulus, training fraction, widths, step limit, and seeds, and no slope interval was computed; we therefore draw no inference from them.\n256:\\paragraph{Threshold and time-grid sensitivity.} The delay is read off a $250$-step grid with a $0.99$ threshold on both accuracies, so it is blind to differences below one probe interval and sensitive to where a run plateaus. The development evaluation shows how this can matter: all six runs of one configuration were censored even though their mean final held-out accuracy was $0.944$ (Appendix~\\ref{sec:si-dev}), and the retained records do not show whether that censoring came from a missed training threshold or a missed held-out threshold, or how close individual runs came to $0.99$. We did not vary the threshold or the grid in the sealed evaluation, so we cannot say how the slope would change under, for example, a $0.95$ threshold, and the retained artifacts do not allow it to be recomputed.\n\\midrule\n16 & 6{,}320 & 2.83 \\\\\n32 & 18{,}784 & 2.00 \\\\\n64 & 62{,}144 & 1.41 \\\\\n128 & 222{,}592 & 1.00 \\\\\n256 & 838{,}400 & 0.71 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Development-plane comparison (descriptive, not final)}\n\\label{sec:si-dev}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cdd964e4-3ba4-4c45-969d-584fa83d6aeb","uuid":"87976b2b-1490-4808-acb8-7452f9af39a0","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:49:21.471Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"\\begin{abstract}\nDelayed generalization (``grokking'') on modular arithmetic is explained through memorization capacity, weight norm, circuit efficiency, or a lazy-to-rich transition, but its dependence on model size has received little systematic measurement.\nWe test one falsifiable prediction in a one-layer transformer trained with AdamW (weight decay $1.0$, full batch) on modular addition modulo $97$: that the delay between fitting the training pairs and generalizing to held-out pairs shrinks as width grows.\nDelay is right-censored at $10{,}000$ steps, and a pre-declared rule reads the 95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval of the slope of $\\ln(\\text{delay})$ against $\\log_2(\\text{width})$ over widths $16$--$256$ with four seeds.\nThe measured slope is $-0.020$ with interval $[-0.585,\\,0.354]$, so the verdict is \\emph{inconclusive}; $40\\%$ of the $20$ runs were censored, and the interval covers about two-fifths of the slopes that the delay floor and cap permit.\nA single-seed reference trainer gives $-0.368$, inside the interval.\nThe evaluation itself was valid, so the result is a calibrated non-answer rather than a failure; we state what it cannot say and which measurements would resolve it.\n\\end{abstract}\n62:\\paragraph{Grokking and its explanations.} \\citet{Power2022GrokkingGB} report that small transformers trained on algorithmically generated tables, including modular arithmetic, reach high held-out accuracy long after fitting the training split. \\citet{Nanda2023ProgressMF} analyze small transformers trained on modular addition mechanistically and define progress measures that track the formation of the generalizing circuit during training. \\citet{Liu2022OmnigrokGB} analyze loss landscapes and weight norm and extend the phenomenon beyond algorithmic data; \\citet{Varma2023ExplainingGT} explain grokking through circuit efficiency and test predictions of that account in training experiments; \\citet{Kumar2023GrokkingAT} relate it to a transition from lazy to rich training dynamics, analyzed in a two-layer regression setting; \\citet{Huang2024UnifiedVO} discuss grokking, double descent, and emergent abilities through the competition of circuits; and \\citet{Davies2023UnifyingGA} place grokking and double descent in one framework. These accounts differ in which quantity should control the delay, and several involve scale-related knobs. Empirical studies have varied some of those knobs on modular arithmetic or related algorithmic tasks: \\citet{Doshi2023ToGO} study modular arithmetic with corrupted labels and examine how generalization and memorization coexist under regularization; \\citet{Thilak2022TheSM} associate grokking under adaptive optimizers with cyclic behavior of the last-layer norm; and \\citet{Prieto2025GrokkingAT} attribute the absence of grokking without regularization to numerical instability in the softmax. We do not re-derive these findings, and none of them has, as far as their stated scope and our non-exhaustive reading indicate, measured delay against width under a fixed protocol, which is the quantity examined here; the present study does not test the accounts against each other.\n62:\\paragraph{Grokking and its explanations.} \\citet{Power2022GrokkingGB} report that small transformers trained on algorithmically generated tables, including modular arithmetic, reach high held-out accuracy long after fitting the training split. \\citet{Nanda2023ProgressMF} analyze small transformers trained on modular addition mechanistically and define progress measures that track the formation of the generalizing circuit during training. \\citet{Liu2022OmnigrokGB} analyze loss landscapes and weight norm and extend the phenomenon beyond algorithmic data; \\citet{Varma2023ExplainingGT} explain grokking through circuit efficiency and test predictions of that account in training experiments; \\citet{Kumar2023GrokkingAT} relate it to a transition from lazy to rich training dynamics, analyzed in a two-layer regression setting; \\citet{Huang2024UnifiedVO} discuss grokking, double descent, and emergent abilities through the competition of circuits; and \\citet{Davies2023UnifyingGA} place grokking and double descent in one framework. These accounts differ in which quantity should control the delay, and several involve scale-related knobs. Empirical studies have varied some of those knobs on modular arithmetic or related algorithmic tasks: \\citet{Doshi2023ToGO} study modular arithmetic with corrupted labels and examine how generalization and memorization coexist under regularization; \\citet{Thilak2022TheSM} associate grokking under adaptive optimizers with cyclic behavior of the last-layer norm; and \\citet{Prieto2025GrokkingAT} attribute the absence of grokking without regularization to numerical instability in the softmax. We do not re-derive these findings, and none of them has, as far as their stated scope and our non-exhaustive reading indicate, measured delay against width under a fixed protocol, which is the quantity examined here; the present study does not test the accounts against each other.\n115:The retained final artifacts consist of the aggregate metrics listed in Section~\\ref{sec:results} and one record per seed (mean validation-accuracy score and wall-clock time). \\emph{Per-width and per-seed delays, fitting and generalization steps, and per-width censoring flags are not retained}, so we cannot show a delay-versus-width plot, and we do not infer one. Separately, a development evaluation on a different data plane (modulus $53$, training fraction $0.5$, widths $32,64,128$, $6{,}000$ steps, two seeds) was used when configuring the evaluated model; its results are reported only as descriptive context in Appendix~\\ref{sec:si-dev} and never mixed with the sealed result.\n229:For context, three configurations that differ cumulatively by the three modifications of Section~\\ref{sec:model} were evaluated on the separate development plane with two seeds each, with scores $0.798$, $0.828$, and $0.850$ and $4$, $6$, and $4$ of $6$ runs censored (Appendix~\\ref{sec:si-dev}). Differences between them are of the same size as the spread between the two seeds of any single configuration, the plane differs from the sealed one in modulus, training fraction, widths, step limit, and seeds, and no slope interval was computed; we therefore draw no inference from them.\n256:\\paragraph{Threshold and time-grid sensitivity.} The delay is read off a $250$-step grid with a $0.99$ threshold on both accuracies, so it is blind to differences below one probe interval and sensitive to where a run plateaus. The development evaluation shows how this can matter: all six runs of one configuration were censored even though their mean final held-out accuracy was $0.944$ (Appendix~\\ref{sec:si-dev}), and the retained records do not show whether that censoring came from a missed training threshold or a missed held-out threshold, or how close individual runs came to $0.99$. We did not vary the threshold or the grid in the sealed evaluation, so we cannot say how the slope would change under, for example, a $0.95$ threshold, and the retained artifacts do not allow it to be recomputed.\n\\midrule\n16 & 6{,}320 & 2.83 \\\\\n32 & 18{,}784 & 2.00 \\\\\n64 & 62{,}144 & 1.41 \\\\\n128 & 222{,}592 & 1.00 \\\\\n256 & 838{,}400 & 0.71 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Development-plane comparison (descriptive, not final)}\n\\label{sec:si-dev}","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
